A stale Angular view often comes from changing data in a way that does not notify the view owner. Mutating `user.name` changes the object internally but keeps the same object reference. If a parent passes that object to an OnPush child, Angular may not see a new input value. A signal has a similar boundary: its default equality uses `Object.is`, so setting the same mutated object reference does not represent a new signal value.

The fix is to update through the owning state API and return a new reference:

```typescript
class ProfileState {
  readonly user = signal({ id: 1, name: 'Mina' });

  rename(name: string) {
    this.user.update(current => ({ ...current, name }));
  }
}
```

For example, a parent template binds `[user]="user()"` to a profile card. `rename('Asha')` creates a new object, the signal publishes it, Angular schedules the view, and the child receives a changed input reference.

The rule is not that JavaScript objects must never be mutated. Local mutable objects can be fine when no reactive boundary depends on their identity. The problem is hidden mutation across a state, input, memoisation, or collection boundary. Unrelated events can make the screen appear to fix itself later, which makes the bug confusing. Treat signal and component input values as replaceable snapshots when their inner data changes; this gives Angular a clear notification and makes state history easier to reason about.

```typescript
import { Component, input, signal } from '@angular/core';

interface User { id: number; name: string; }

@Component({
  selector: 'profile-card',
  template: '<p>User: {{ user().name }}</p>',
})
class ProfileCard { readonly user = input.required<User>(); }

@Component({
  selector: 'profile-page',
  imports: [ProfileCard],
  template: `
    <profile-card [user]="user()" />
    <button (click)="rename('Asha')">Rename</button>
  `,
})
class ProfilePage {
  readonly user = signal<User>({ id: 1, name: 'Mina' });
  rename(name: string) {
    this.user.update(current => ({ ...current, name }));
  }
}
```

The `track` expression in an Angular `@for` block defines item identity. Angular evaluates it for each item and uses the key to match the new collection with existing repeated views. That match lets Angular keep the right DOM element and child component instance when an item moves, disappears, or is inserted.

A dynamic list should normally track a stable unique field:

```typescript
template: `
  @for (task of tasks(); track task.id) {
    <task-row [task]="task" />
  } @empty { <p>No tasks.</p> }
`
```

For example, inserting a new task at the top changes every array index, but existing task IDs stay the same. Angular creates one new row and can reuse the other rows under their original identities. This preserves focus, input state, and child-component state with the correct task.

Tracking by `$index` is reasonable for a truly static collection whose order and membership never change. It is unsafe for editable or sorted data because a position is not an item identity. Tracking by the object itself also depends on receiving the same object instances; mapping an API response into fresh objects can make every item appear new. Keys must be unique within the collection and stable for the logical lifetime of an item. `track` is therefore a correctness contract for view identity as well as a performance aid.

```typescript
import { Component, input, signal } from '@angular/core';

interface Task { id: number; title: string; }

@Component({ selector: 'task-row', template: '<label>{{ task().title }} <input></label>' })
class TaskRow { readonly task = input.required<Task>(); }

@Component({
  selector: 'task-list',
  imports: [TaskRow],
  template: `
    <button (click)="prepend()">Add first task</button>
    @for (task of tasks(); track task.id) {
      <task-row [task]="task" />
    } @empty { <p>No tasks.</p> }
  `,
})
class TaskList {
  readonly tasks = signal<Task[]>([{ id: 10, title: 'Read' }, { id: 20, title: 'Practise' }]);
  prepend() {
    this.tasks.update(items => [{ id: 5, title: 'Plan' }, ...items]);
  }
}
```

A subscription leak happens when a long-lived Observable still has a subscriber after the owning component is gone. The source may continue doing work, and its callback can retain component state. Angular provides two clear lifetime-aware patterns.

For values used only by the template, `AsyncPipe` is usually simplest. It subscribes to the Observable, exposes the latest value, marks the component when a new value arrives, and unsubscribes when the component is destroyed or the bound Observable reference changes. For imperative side effects, `takeUntilDestroyed()` completes the pipeline when the current injection context is destroyed.

For example:

```typescript
class StatusPanel {
  readonly clock = interval(1000); // template: {{ clock | async }}

  constructor(private notifications: Notifications) {
    this.notifications.events
      .pipe(takeUntilDestroyed())
      .subscribe(message => this.show(message));
  }

  private show(message: string) {}
}
```

The no-argument form needs an injection context, such as construction. Code elsewhere can inject a `DestroyRef` earlier and pass it to the operator. Not every subscription leaks: a finite Observable that completes releases its subscriber, and ordinary HTTP requests usually complete after their response. Even then, cancellation can prevent outdated work. Avoid creating unmanaged nested subscriptions; keep ownership visible and choose the cleanup boundary that matches the consumer's lifetime.

```typescript
import { AsyncPipe } from '@angular/common';
import { Component, Injectable, signal } from '@angular/core';
import { takeUntilDestroyed } from '@angular/core/rxjs-interop';
import { interval, Subject } from 'rxjs';

@Injectable({ providedIn: 'root' })
class Notifications { readonly events = new Subject<string>(); }

@Component({
  selector: 'status-panel',
  imports: [AsyncPipe],
  template: `<p>Seconds: {{ seconds | async }}</p><p>{{ latest() }}</p>`,
})
class StatusPanel {
  readonly seconds = interval(1000);
  readonly latest = signal('No notification');

  constructor(notifications: Notifications) {
    notifications.events
      .pipe(takeUntilDestroyed())
      .subscribe(message => this.latest.set(message));
  }
}
```
